      "text": "<think>\nTo determine which option cannot support the positive side's point of view, we need to consider the arguments made by both sides of the debate.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe positive side's point of view is that tourism explorers who commit intentional adventures should be charged for the deployment of public resources to rescue them. This is based on the idea that the taxpayers should bear the cost of rescue operations, as they are the ones who fund the tourism industry and benefit from it.\nThe negative side's point of view is that rescue operations should not be charged, as it would be unfair to other taxpayers who are not involved in intentional adventures.\nNow, let's analyze each option:\nA) A rational citizen should be responsible for his own actions; otherwise, it will be unfair to other taxpayers.\n- This option supports the positive side's point of view. If taxpayers are not responsible for the actions of tourists, it would be unfair to other taxpayers who are not involved in intentional adventures.\nB) The establishment of a professional rescue system requires a certain amount of financial support.\n- This option supports the positive side's point of view. The professional rescue system needs financial support to function effectively, and charging for rescue operations would reduce the amount of money available for such a system.\nC) It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress. simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility.\n- This option does not support the positive side's point of view. Instead, it suggests that charging for rescue operations would not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility, which is the opposite of the positive side's argument.\nD) Tourist explorers who illegally enter unsafe areas and waste social resources and taxpayers' money should bear the corresponding expenses.\n- This option supports the positive side's point of view. If tourist explorers are not responsible for their actions, they should bear the cost of rescue operations, which aligns with the positive side's argument.\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress. simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility.\n</answer>",
